/! What I Learned					
section
	.container
		.row
			.col-lg-12
				h2 What I learned
				p.lead.mb-3 Working on Jumpix gave me early experience contributing to a digital product from multiple perspectives and seeing how an idea evolves into a customer-facing experience. Collaborating closely with the CEO, founders, and CTO taught me how business goals, technical requirements, marketing priorities, and user needs influence product decisions, especially within an early-stage startup where ideas and priorities can change quickly.
				p.lead Working alongside the CTO on front-end development also strengthened my understanding of the relationship between design and technology. Translating concepts and mockups into responsive experiences using Ruby on Rails, Slim, SCSS, JavaScript, and Bootstrap taught me to consider technical constraints while designing and to think beyond how an interface looks to how it actually functions for the user.
				p.lead Planning and coordinating user research was another important part of the experience. Hearing directly from participants helped me understand the difference between assumptions we made while building the product and what users actually valued. Their feedback validated areas of the experience that were working while identifying opportunities around file viewing, mobile access, accessibility, and other features that could shape future development.
				p.lead Jumpix also taught me how much work surrounds the product itself. Supporting marketing, recruiting, events, user guides, motion graphics, and other launch initiatives gave me a broader understanding of how a product is communicated and introduced to an audience. It reinforced the importance of creating a consistent experience across the application and the touchpoints that help people discover, understand, and begin using it.
				//.squiggle.justify-content-start.align-items-start
				.squiggle-vertical					
					
					
				
